This is the first of two tutorials on `mim`, the Morloc installation manager.
Part 1 takes you from an empty machine to a working cross-language Morloc
program. link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] picks up from the same program
and covers installing, serving, and shipping it.

You need a Linux machine (x86-64 or ARM) or an Apple Silicon Mac. On Windows,
work inside WSL. Nothing else is assumed: no Morloc, no compilers, no conda,
no container engine.

== Why mim exists

A Morloc program is a composition of functions written in different languages.
A single program can pull `roll` out of R, `best` out of {cpp}, and `narrate`
out of Python, and the compiler will type-check the composition across all
three. To build it, the machine needs a Morloc compiler, a Morloc runtime, an R
interpreter, a {cpp} toolchain, a Python interpreter, and every library those
three need -- all of them mutually compatible, because the pools link against a
shared runtime and hand values to each other at run time.

`mim` owns that problem. It provisions the compiler and runtime, solves the
whole cross-language dependency world with conda/pixi, and keeps the result in
a named, isolated *environment*. There is no separate Morloc install step:
installing `mim` and creating an environment is the whole of it.

Three words get used precisely in this tutorial, and they are three different
things:

*module*:: Morloc source. `import random` imports a module.
*package*:: a language dependency -- a conda package, a PyPI wheel, a crate.
*environment*:: what `mim` manages: a solved toolchain plus a data directory.

== Installing mim

One command, on Linux (x86-64 or ARM) or Apple Silicon macOS:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/morloc-project/morloc-manager/main/scripts/install.sh | sh
----

This downloads the prebuilt `mim` for your platform, checks it against the
published SHA-256 when that sidecar is reachable, and installs it into
`~/.local/bin` (or `$XDG_BIN_HOME`, if you set it). No `sudo` is needed. Two
environment variables adjust it:

[cols="1,4"]
|===
| Variable | Effect

| `MIM_DEST`
| Directory to install into. Default: `$XDG_BIN_HOME`, else `~/.local/bin`.

| `MIM_VERSION`
| Git tag to install, e.g. `v0.28.0`. Default: the latest release.
|===

The installer never edits your shell startup files. When the destination is
already on your `PATH`, you are done:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim --version
mim 0.28.0
----

Otherwise it prints the exact command to add the directory to your `PATH`.
This is the common case on macOS, whose default `PATH` comes from `/etc/paths`
and does not include `~/.local/bin`.

`mim` also ships as a plain binary on the
https://github.com/morloc-project/morloc-manager/releases[morloc-manager
releases page]. Note that it lives in its own repository, not in the `morloc`
repository. If you download it with a browser on macOS, clear the quarantine
flag first:

[source, console]
----
$ xattr -d com.apple.quarantine mim
----

`mim` is a single static binary with nothing to link against, but it does shell
out to a few standard tools: `curl` (required -- `wget` is not a substitute),
`tar`, `nix` on NixOS only, and a container engine only if your host cannot run
the native backend.

== Choosing a backend

`mim` has two backends and picks one for you on most machines.

*Native* is the default wherever it works. Morloc runs directly on your host
against a conda/pixi toolchain that `mim` provisions into a private directory.
Nothing is installed system-wide and no container engine is needed. It works on
glibc Linux with a standard filesystem layout, on Apple Silicon macOS, and on
NixOS provided `nix` is present and unprivileged user namespaces are enabled
(conda binaries expect a dynamic loader at a path NixOS does not have, so `mim`
builds a `buildFHSEnv` sandbox to supply one).

*Container* is the fallback: musl distributions such as Alpine, hosts with a
non-standard layout, NixOS without `nix`, and Intel macOS, for which no
prebuilt Morloc compiler is published. Docker and Podman are supported.

`mim new` probes the host and remembers the choice for later environments. To
force one:

[source, console]
----
$ mim new --engine none      # native
$ mim new --engine podman    # or: --engine docker
----

If more than one container engine is installed and no default has been recorded
yet, `mim` asks rather than guessing.

[WARNING]
.Experimental Feature
====

`--engine apptainer` (equivalently `--engine singularity`) is the least tested
backend and creating an environment with it is not currently expected to
succeed: the image build emits a Dockerfile, which Apptainer cannot consume.
The SLURM dispatch described in <<slurm-dispatch>> depends on
it. Use the native backend, Docker, or Podman for everything in this tutorial.

====

== Creating an environment

An *environment* is a named, self-contained Morloc installation: a solved
toolchain of compilers and language runtimes, the Morloc compiler and runtime
built against it, and a data directory holding modules, installed programs, and
build caches. Everything Morloc does happens inside one, and environments do
not interfere with each other or with anything else on your machine.

Create one and call it `dnd`, since a dice roller is what you are about to
build:

[source, console]
----
$ mim new --non-interactive dnd
Provisioning morloc runtime from release v0.100.2...
Provisioning pixi 0.76.2...
Solving native toolchain with pixi (this may take a few minutes)...
...
Native environment 'dnd' is ready.
Set 'dnd' as the default environment.
----

// REVIEWER: this transcript is assembled from mim's format strings
// (provision.rs:664, provision.rs:758, main.rs materialize_native_env, and
// finalize_new_env). It matches what getting-started.asc in the docs repo
// records for a real run on 2026-09-02. Worth one confirming run.

The first `new` is the slow one: `mim` downloads the Morloc compiler for your
platform, solves a conda toolchain, and builds the Morloc runtime from source
against it. Budget several minutes. Later environments reuse the downloaded
compiler, and re-running `new` or `update` with unchanged requirements skips
the solve entirely.

Drop `--non-interactive` and `mim` walks you through the same settings as
prompts -- version, backend, languages, packages, scope, home, name, default --
and offers to save your answers as a JSON file at the end. That file is a
record for now; there is no importer yet.

Without a name, an environment is named after the Morloc version it tracks:
`latest`, or `v0.98.0` for a pinned `--morloc-version`. The first environment
you create becomes the *default* -- the one every command targets when you do
not pass `--env` -- so `dnd` is ready to use immediately.

No language toolchain is installed up front. Python, R, {cpp}, and Rust are
provisioned on demand, the first time you build a program that uses them. That
is why the environment came up so much smaller than you might expect, and why
your first `morloc make` will pause. If you would rather have a language
present whether or not any program asks for it -- a general workbench rather
than a project environment -- pin it:

[source, console]
----
$ mim new dnd --lang py,cpp
$ mim new dnd --lang py@3.12 --lang r     # equivalent form, with a version pin
----

== Working inside an environment

Two ways in. `mim run` executes one command:

[source, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc --version
0.100.2
----

The `--` matters. Without it, flags like `-o` or `--version` are eaten by `mim
run` instead of reaching the command you meant to run.

`mim shell` opens an interactive session instead:

[source, console]
----
$ mim shell
----

Inside that shell `morloc` is on your `PATH` and the prompt is tagged with the
environment name. Everything from here on is written as if you are in a `mim
shell`; outside one, prefix each command with `mim run --`.

The shell starts in your current working directory and changes you make there
persist -- on the container backends that directory is bind-mounted in at
`/work`, and on the native backend it is simply your own filesystem. Either
way, edit files with your normal editor on the host and build them inside the
environment.

== Your first program

Make a directory and write a Python die roller. This is ordinary Python: it
imports nothing from Morloc and knows nothing about it.

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]
----

Now the Morloc module that gives it a type and a name:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (roll, rollAdv)

import root-py
import random

source Py from "dice.py" ("roll")

--' Roll n dice with d pips each
roll ::
  --' number of dice
  Int ->
  --' pips per die
  Int ->
  <Random> [Int]

--' Roll 2d20 and keep the higher die
rollAdv :: <Random> Int
rollAdv = do
  xs <- roll 2 20
  fold1 max xs
----

Line by line:

* `module dnd (roll, rollAdv)` names the module and lists what it exports.
  Exported terms become subcommands of the compiled program.
* `import root-py` brings in the standard library's Python-backed core
  (`max`, `fold1`, and the rest). `import random` brings in the random-number
  module, which is where the `Random` effect is declared.
* `source Py from "dice.py" ("roll")` pulls one name out of a Python file. The
  tag `Py` tells the compiler which toolchain and which pool it belongs to.
* `roll :: Int -> Int -> <Random> [Int]` is the general signature, written here
  with a docstring per argument. Morloc checks calls against this signature,
  not against anything in the Python.
* `<Random>` is an *effect*: it marks `roll` as non-deterministic, so the type
  system knows two calls may disagree. A function that calls `roll` must either
  declare the effect too or handle it.
* `--'` is a docstring. A plain `--` comment is discarded; `--'` attaches
  documentation to the thing below it, and that documentation reaches the
  generated command line interface.

Build it:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make dnd.loc
Provisioning environment dependencies (.../mim sync)...
Environment dependencies synced for 'dnd'.
----

Two things worth noticing about that build.

First, you never installed the standard library. `morloc make` auto-installs
any imported module that is missing, so `import root-py` and `import random`
fetched `root`, `random`, and their dependencies for you. (`mim new` also
deposits a version-matched pin file so the whole environment agrees on one
commit per module; `--offline` on `morloc make` turns auto-install off.)

Second, that "Provisioning environment dependencies" line is `morloc` calling
back out to `mim`. Before compiling any pool, the compiler writes out what the
program needs and hands it to `mim sync`, which folds those requirements into
the environment's solved world. On a first build in a fresh environment this is
where Python gets installed. Section <<declaring-dependencies>> is about what
you can put in that request.

The build produced two things next to your source:

`dnd`:: a launcher, named after the *source file*. Override with `-o`.
`dnd-build/`:: the build directory: `manifest.json`, `envspec.json`, and a
  compiled pool per language under `pools/<lang>/`.

The launcher is a thin shell script that execs the shared `morloc-nexus`
runtime against `manifest.json`. The nexus parses arguments, starts whichever
language pools the call needs, routes data between them, and prints the result.

Run it:

[source, console]
----
$ ./dnd roll 3 6
[5,3,6]
$ ./dnd rollAdv
5
----

You wrote no argument parser and no usage text, but you have both:

[source, console]
----
$ ./dnd -h
Usage: ./dnd <nexus_options> <command> <command_options>

Commands:
  roll     Roll n dice with d pips each
  rollAdv  Roll 2d20 and keep the higher die

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./dnd roll -h
Roll n dice with d pips each

Usage: ./dnd <nexus_options> roll <command_options>

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')

Positional arguments:
  1:  number of dice
      type: Int
  2:  pips per die
      type: Int

Return: [Int]
----

Every word of that came from your docstrings and your types. The command line
interface is not something you build in Morloc; it is a *view* of the library
you wrote. Part 2 uses two more views of the same library -- an HTTP API and an
MCP server -- without changing a line of it.

== Adding two more languages

The point of Morloc is that an implementation can move to another language
without the composition changing. Rewrite `roll` in R, add a `best` in {cpp},
and keep Python for the narration.

[source, r]
.dice.R
----
roll <- function(n, d) {
    sample(1:d, n, replace = TRUE)
}
----

[source, cpp]
.dice.hpp
----
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

template <typename A>
A best(const std::vector<A>& xs) {
    return *std::max_element(xs.begin(), xs.end());
}
----

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
def narrate(n):
    return f"You rolled a {n}"
----

None of those three files knows about Morloc. The module ties them together:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (roll, rollAdv)

import root-r
import root-cpp
import root-py
import random

source R   from "dice.R"   ("roll")
source Cpp from "dice.hpp" ("best")
source Py  from "dice.py"  ("narrate")

--' Roll n dice with d pips each
roll ::
  --' number of dice
  Int ->
  --' pips per die
  Int ->
  <Random> [Int]

best :: [Int] -> Int
narrate :: Int -> Str

--' Roll 2d20 and keep the higher die
rollAdv :: <Random> Str
rollAdv = do
  xs <- roll 2 20
  narrate (best xs)
----

Rebuild. This is where on-demand provisioning earns its keep: the program now
needs an R interpreter and a {cpp} toolchain that neither you nor `mim new`
asked for, so the first build after this edit pauses to solve and install them.

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make dnd.loc
Provisioning environment dependencies (.../mim sync)...
Environment dependencies synced for 'dnd'.
...
$ ls dnd-build/pools
cpp  py  r
$ ./dnd rollAdv
"You rolled a 10"
$ ./dnd roll 4 6
[3,6,6,6]
----

// REVIEWER: the elided line hides the g++ command line the cpp pool build
// echoes. It is real but it is full of absolute paths, so per the docs
// STYLE.md rule about churn I cut it rather than date the tutorial.

Three pools now, and `rollAdv` crosses all three: R rolls the dice, {cpp} picks
the maximum, Python formats the sentence. You changed no composition -- only
which language each name came from -- and the nexus handles the serialization
at every boundary.

[[declaring-dependencies]]
== Declaring dependencies

Real programs need libraries. Morloc packages carry their language dependencies
in a `package.yaml` next to the main `.loc` file. Create one:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc new dnd
Created package.yaml for 'dnd'
----

NOTE: `morloc new` (inside the environment) creates a *package*. `mim new` (on
the host) creates an *environment*. They are unrelated.

The generated file is metadata only. Dependencies go in a per-language map:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
name: dnd
version: 0.1.0
synopsis: A cross-language dice roller
license: MIT

py-deps:
  numpy:
    version: ">=2,<3"
    source: conda
r-deps:
  jsonlite: "*"

lang-versions:
  py: ">=3.11"
----

Each language has its own key -- `py-deps`, `r-deps`, `cpp-deps`, `rust-deps`,
`julia-deps` -- and each entry is either the long form above or the shorthand
`name: "<constraint>"`. Constraints use conda match-spec syntax: `"*"` for
anything, `">=2,<3"` for an interval, `"1.26"` for a series.

The shorthand only works where the language has an unambiguous default
registry, and Python does not have one:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc envspec dnd.loc
morloc: user error (dependency 'numpy' must declare a source for py (one of: conda, pypi))
----

[cols="1,2,1", options="header"]
|===
| Language | Sources honored | Default when omitted

| `py`    | `conda`, `pypi` | none -- you must say
| `r`     | `conda`         | `conda`
| `cpp`   | `conda`         | `conda`
| `rust`  | `crates`        | `crates`
| `julia` | `pkg`           | `pkg`
|===

`lang-versions` constrains the toolchain itself rather than a library, so
`py: ">=3.11"` pins the interpreter.

`morloc envspec` prints exactly what the compiler will hand to `mim sync`,
without building anything. It is the fastest way to check that a `package.yaml`
says what you meant:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc envspec dnd.loc
----

[source, json]
----
{
  "envspec_version": 2,
  "morloc_version": "0.100.2",
  "languages": [
    { "lang": "r" },
    { "lang": "cpp" },
    { "lang": "py", "constraint": ">=3.11" }
  ],
  "packages": {
    "py": [ { "name": "numpy", "constraint": ">=2,<3", "source": "conda" } ],
    "r":  [ { "name": "jsonlite", "constraint": "*", "source": "conda" } ]
  },
  "system": [],
  "modules": []
}
----

// REVIEWER: real output, reflowed for width -- `morloc envspec` prints it on
// one line. The `r-deps` shorthand picking up `"source":"conda"` is the
// defaulting table above, working.
//
// REVIEWER: the cpp entry above is the pre-fix capture. Merely *having* a
// package.yaml used to drop `"std":"c++20"` from it (two disagreeing defaults
// for `cpp-version`); filed as report 0004 and fixed in
// Morloc/Namespace/State.hs, but NOT yet built. Once you build, the cpp entry
// becomes {"lang":"cpp","std":"c++20"} in both cases -- re-capture this JSON
// then. Nothing in the prose depends on it.

Requirements are unioned across the whole import graph, so a module you import
brings its own dependencies with it and you do not restate them. When several
modules constrain the same package, `mim` solves one world that satisfies all of
them, or fails and tells you which constraints conflict. The world for the whole
environment is one solve, not one per program -- so an environment cannot end up
with two incompatible NumPy versions, and two of your programs cannot disagree.

To see what the solve produced:

[source, console]
----
$ mim info dnd --packages
----

Finally, `include` controls which files are bundled when the package is
installed:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
include:
  - "dnd.loc"
  - "dice.py"
  - "dice.R"
  - "dice.hpp"
----

Without `include`, everything in the project directory is copied, filtered by
`.morlocignore`. With it, every file named by a `source ... from "path"`
declaration must be covered by some entry, or the install fails. Entries are
exact paths, directories with a trailing slash (`"src/"`), or globs (`"*.py"`,
`"**/*.R"`). Paths resolve against the directory holding `package.yaml`;
absolute paths and `..` are rejected, and the relative layout is preserved on
install. Transitive references -- one R file calling `source()` on another --
are not discovered for you, so list them yourself.

== Adding tools to the environment

Program dependencies belong in `package.yaml`. Tools that *you* want in the
environment -- `jq`, `ripgrep`, a profiler, a language server -- belong to the
environment, and are supplied as a file with one package per line (`#` comments
and blanks ignored):

[source, console]
----
$ cat > tools.conda <<'EOF'
jq
ripgrep
hyperfine>=1.18
EOF
$ mim modify --env dnd --conda-packages-file tools.conda
$ mim run -- jq --version
----

Conda packages are folded into the same solve as everything else and work on
every backend. There is a second, narrower channel for things conda-forge does
not carry -- `locales`, kernel-matched tooling -- which are installed with the
base image's `apt` and therefore work on container backends only:

[source, console]
----
$ mim modify --env dnd --system-packages-file tools.apt
----

Both flags are also accepted by `mim new`. The file is the *whole* list for that
source: re-applying it replaces the stored list, so to add or drop one package
you edit the file and re-apply. Either change triggers a rebuild at the current
Morloc version, and the stored list is updated only after the rebuild succeeds
-- a typo that breaks the build leaves the previous list intact rather than
wedging the environment.

== Managing environments

You will accumulate environments: one per project, one to try a new compiler
release, one to reproduce a colleague's bug.

[source, console]
----
$ mim ls                                   # all environments; default marked
$ mim info dnd                             # detail on one
$ mim info dnd --packages                  # every package in the solved world
$ mim run --env other -- morloc --version  # act on a non-default environment
$ mim modify --env other --set-default     # change the default
$ mim rm other                             # remove one
$ mim nuke                                 # remove all of them
----

`mim info <name>` reports an environment as four groups -- identity, the folders
it occupies, the variables a run sees, and its dependencies:

[source, console]
----
$ mim info dnd
Name:      dnd
Scope:     local
Default:   yes
Backend:   native
Morloc:    0.100.2
Status:    materialized

Folders (host):
  Data dir:     .../environments/dnd
  Runtime:      .../environments/dnd  (shared with state)
  Pixi:         .../environments/dnd/pixi
  Requirements: .../environments/dnd/requirements
  Cache:        .../environments/dnd/cache
  Config:       .../environments/dnd/env.yaml

Environment (exported into run/serve):
  MORLOC_HOME=.../environments/dnd
  MORLOC_ENV=dnd
  MORLOC_PIXI=.../bin/pixi

Dependencies (locked):
  Languages:  python 3.12.4 (on demand), r 4.3.3 (on demand)
  ... packages in the solved world (`info dnd --packages` for the full list)
----

// REVIEWER: source-derived from the `Cmd::Info` printer in main.rs (the
// labels and column widths are exact; the values are placeholders). Package
// counts and language versions churn, so I elided them per STYLE.md.

Two verbs cover the lifecycle after creation, split by what they touch.
`update` rebuilds; with no flags it re-solves at the environment's *current*
Morloc version and never moves the version implicitly:

[source, console]
----
$ mim update --env dnd                          # rebuild, same version
$ mim update --env dnd --latest                 # move to the newest release
$ mim update --env dnd --morloc-version 0.98.0  # move to a specific version
$ mim update --env dnd --force                  # fresh re-solve (repair)
----

`modify` changes settings without moving the version. Setting the default is
instant; changing languages or packages triggers a rebuild. It validates the
whole request before changing anything, so an invalid combination never leaves
an environment half-modified.

=== A home that outlives the environment

Under Docker and Podman a container runs as your host user but does *not* mount
your host `$HOME`. Each environment owns a private home directory instead,
writable from both sides, which `mim info` prints under `Folders`. Drop a
`.bashrc` there and the next `mim shell` picks it up. `--dotfiles <dir>`
recursively copies a directory into that home, overwriting like `cp -rf`:

[source, console]
----
$ mim modify --env dnd --dotfiles ~/dotfiles
----

That home is owned by the environment and is deleted with it. When you want the
home itself to survive -- notes, scratch data, shell history -- mount a host
directory as `$HOME` instead:

[source, console]
----
$ mim new dnd --mount-home ~/morloc-homes/dnd
$ mim modify --env dnd --mount-home ~/morloc-homes/dnd   # or set it later
$ mim modify --env dnd --mount-home none                 # back to the env's own
----

The directory is created if absent and is only ever mounted: `mim rm` deletes
the environment and leaves that directory alone, so a rebuilt environment picks
up where the last one left off.

Two things to keep in mind. Every program you run in the environment gets full
read/write access to the mounted directory, so mounting your real `$HOME` hands
the environment your whole account; a dedicated directory is the better trade.
And `--dotfiles` and `--mount-home` are mutually exclusive, because copying
dotfiles over a directory you own would overwrite your own files. Both are
Docker/Podman only: Apptainer already mounts the host `$HOME`, and the native
backend runs against your real one.

== Checking health

`mim doctor` runs read-only checks and exits non-zero if any fails, so it fits
a setup script or a CI gate.

[source, console]
----
$ mim doctor
$ mim doctor --env dnd --deep      # slower checks; on a container backend
                                   # these run inside it
$ mim doctor --strict              # treat warnings as failures
$ mim doctor --json                # machine-readable report
----

It is grouped the way `info` is. On the native backend: prerequisites (data
directories, the CA bundle, the runtime record, the captured toolchain, pixi),
runtime (core artifacts, per-language stacks, library linkage), versions
(compiler and manager in step), dependencies (the world is solved), manifests,
and then the deep checks, which exercise the installed program launchers. Each
line is `[ok]`, `[!!]`, `[EE]`, or `[--]` for a skipped check, and the last
line counts them: so many passed, so many warnings, so many errors.

Reach for `doctor` when a build fails in a way that does not look like your
code -- a missing shared library, a stale runtime after an interrupted update,
a half-built image. `mim update --force` is the usual repair.

== Fetching example programs

`mim demos` downloads the *morloc-dungeon*: a curated set of example programs,
published per Morloc version, every one of which is known to build and pass on
that version.

[source, console]
----
$ mim demos --list                 # look without downloading
$ mim demos                        # all demos for the active env's version
$ mim demos --tag rust-examples    # just one group
----

Demos land in `examples-<tag>-<version>/` in the current directory.

[NOTE]
====

Nothing is published yet. As of 2026-09-02 the command reports
`Environment error: no demos are published yet`, because the dungeon
repository has no releases.

