[[slurm-dispatch]]
== Dispatching work to a cluster

[WARNING]
.Experimental Feature
====

SLURM dispatch is unfinished and is blocked behind Apptainer support, which
does not currently create environments. Read this section as a design sketch,
not as instructions.

====

Prefix a call in a `.loc` file with a label, and that call can be redirected to
a SLURM-managed compute node instead of running in the driver process:

[source, morloc]
----
foo x = big:myjob x
----

The label names a resource group defined in a sidecar YAML next to the `.loc`
file:

[source, yaml]
.heavy.yaml
----
labeled-groups:
  big:
    remote:
      threads: 8
      memory: 32        # GB
      time: 3600        # seconds
      gpus: 0
----

The presence of `remote:` is what activates dispatch. The compiler emits the
remote-call form only when the build configuration says so
(`mim update --reinit --init-arg --slurm`), and `mim run --slurm-bridge` opens
a Unix socket the container uses to reach the host's `sbatch`/`sacct` -- the
container itself never needs the SLURM clients. Compute nodes re-enter the same
environment through the same `mim`, so the driver and the workers run identical
images. Arguments flow by reference through a content-addressed
`.morloc-cache/` directory, so a large input is not copied into the job
submission, and an unchanged re-run hits the memoized result instead of
resubmitting.

Before relying on any of this on a new cluster:

[source, console]
----
$ mim doctor --slurm
----

which checks that `sbatch`/`sacct` are reachable, that the build config has
`slurm-support`, that the environment's image resolves, that the `mim` binary
sits at a path every compute node can see (typically `~/.local/bin` on an
NFS-shared `$HOME`), and that the runtime directory is writable.

// REVIEWER: I cut roughly three pages here -- the old tutorial walked through
// the seven-step bridge protocol in detail. That belongs in an internals
// chapter, not in a tutorial for a feature that cannot currently be run. Say
// the word and I will restore it, or move it to the manual's internals
// chapter.

== What is next

You can now install `mim`, create and manage environments, write a Morloc
program that spans Python, R, and {cpp}, declare its dependencies, add tools,
and diagnose an environment that has gone wrong.

link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] takes the same `dnd` program and makes it
a service: installed into the environment, exposed over MCP and an HTTP API,
served, and -- experimentally -- frozen into a portable container image.

